# Light and Optics: Reflection, Refraction, and Lenses

> Light travels in straight lines until a surface turns it. This
> lesson follows reflection, refraction, total internal reflection,
> lenses, dispersion, and the defects of the human eye.

## 1. Reflection and Plane Mirrors

Light travels in straight lines — the reason shadows are sharp
and eclipses are predictable. When it strikes a surface it
**reflects**, obeying two laws: the angle of incidence equals the
angle of reflection, and incident ray, normal, and reflected ray
lie in one plane.

A **plane mirror** forms an image that is **virtual**, erect, as
far behind the mirror as the object is in front, and **laterally
inverted** — your left hand appears as the right hand.

[tip]
To read the mirrored writing on the front of an ambulance,
hold it to a mirror — lateral inversion reverses it back.
[/tip]

## 2. Spherical Mirrors

A **concave mirror** converges light; a **convex mirror** diverges
it. The **focal length** f is half the radius of curvature.

| Feature | Concave | Convex |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Reflecting face | inner (curved in) | outer (bulging out) |
| Image | real or virtual, magnified or small | always virtual, small, erect |
| Field of view | narrow | wide |
| Uses | dentist's mirror, headlights, solar furnaces, shaving mirrors | vehicle rear-view and street mirrors |

@math label="eq:mirror-formula"
\frac{1}{v} + \frac{1}{u} = \frac{1}{f}
@/math

The **magnification** m = −v/u; its sign reveals whether the
image is inverted (real) or erect (virtual).

[example]
An object 30 cm from a concave mirror of focal length 20 cm:
1/v = 1/20 − 1/30 = 1/60, so v = 60 cm — a real image twice
the size (m = −60/30 = −2).
[/example]

## 3. Refraction and Refractive Index

Light bends when it crosses obliquely into a medium of different
**optical density** — the straw that seems broken at the water
line. The **refractive index** compares speeds:

@math label="eq:refractive-index"
n = \frac{c}{v}
@/math

@chart bar title="Refractive index of common media"
labels:
Water
Crown glass
Flint glass
Diamond

data:
1.33
1.52
1.62
2.42
@/chart

Air's shifting layers make stars **twinkle**, and the Sun is seen
a few minutes before sunrise and after sunset for the same
reason — atmospheric refraction.

## 4. Total Internal Reflection

Beyond the **critical angle**, light striking from the denser
side cannot escape and reflects totally. A diamond's cut exploits
its tiny critical angle (~24°) to send light back to the eye,
creating brilliance. An **optical fibre** pipes light along a bent
glass thread the same way — the backbone of internet cables. On a
hot road, refracting air layers fabricate a **mirage** of water.

## 5. Lenses

A **convex (converging) lens** bulges out and brings rays to a
focus; a **concave (diverging) lens** hollows in and spreads them.

| Feature | Convex lens | Concave lens |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Shape | thicker at the middle | thinner at the middle |
| Focus | real — rays meet | virtual — rays appear to meet |
| Image | real and inverted or virtual and magnified | always virtual, erect, small |
| Uses | magnifying glass, camera, projector | correcting short sight |

@math label="eq:lens-formula"
\frac{1}{v} - \frac{1}{u} = \frac{1}{f}
@/math

The **power** of a lens, P = 1/f (f in metres), is measured in
**dioptres**: a 25 cm focal-length converging lens has P = +4 D.

## 6. Dispersion and the Rainbow

A prism splits white light into the spectrum **ROYGBIV** — red
deviated least, violet most — because refractive index varies
with frequency. A **rainbow** repeats the prism trick inside
raindrops: refraction on entry, one internal reflection, refraction
on exit — always seen opposite the Sun.

@diagram tree id="light-at-boundaries" layout="tree" aria="Light behaviour at boundaries: reflection, refraction, and dispersion"
  "Light meets a boundary" -> "Reflection: mirrors"
  "Light meets a boundary" -> "Refraction: bent path, straw in water"
  "Refraction: bent path" -> "Dispersion: prism spectrum"
  "Refraction: bent path" -> "Total internal reflection: fibres"
@/diagram

## 7. The Human Eye and Its Defects

The eye's lens focuses on the **retina**; the **least distance of
distinct vision** is about 25 cm.

| Defect | Problem | Remedy |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Myopia (short sight) | image forms before the retina | concave (diverging) lens |
| Hypermetropia (long sight) | image forms behind the retina | convex (converging) lens |
| Presbyopia | ageing lens loses flexibility | bifocal lenses |

## 8. Quick Revision

| Item | Remember |
| --- | --- |
| Mirror formula | 1/v + 1/u = 1/f; m = −v/u |
| Vehicle mirrors | convex — wide field of view |
| Refractive index | n = c/v; diamond 2.42, water 1.33 |
| Optical fibre | works by total internal reflection |
| Lens power | P = 1/f (metres), unit dioptre |
| Myopia | corrected with a concave lens |
